    "updated_at": "2023-09-08T07:28:57Z",
    "body": "The core question asked in https://github.com/validator/validator/issues/1332 is: _Why is flow content now allowed in  `button` but is allowed in `a`?_ Maybe there’s an obvious answer to that which I’m just not aware of. But I can understand why a developer might see that difference between the content models for `button` and `a` as an inconsistency.\r\n\r\nTo put it in other terms, one imaginable general articulation of the use cases for allowing flow content in `button` is: For similar reasons to those allowing flow content in `a`.\r\n\r\nI have no strong opinion on it either way, except to say the HTML checker’s core purpose is helping developers statically catch _unintentional_ markup mistakes — for example, catch cases where, say, they misplaced a `</button>` end tag and so they ended up with content that was meant to follow the button being instead unintentionally part of the button.\r\n\r\nAnother purpose of the HTML checker it to not cause developers to end up ignoring the checker — or stop using it completely — because they get annoyed and frustrated when they’re _intentionally_ using markup in some (currently) non-conforming way (as long as it doesn’t have adverse effect on accessibility or have other problems among those listed in https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/introduction.html#restrictions-on-content-models-and-on-attribute-values), and so the checker is reporting something to them as an error that they don’t perceive as a valid error.\r\n\r\nIn general there’s always a tradeoff between the purpose of being to try and help developers catch markup mistakes and the purpose of the checker behavior not being overly prescriptive to the point that it causes developers to get frustrated and annoyed to the degree that they stop using the checker. \r\n\r\nBut I don’t personally know enough about the use cases and deployed content to understand whether the no-flow-content-in-button restriction might be something a lot of developers in practice see as an overly prescriptive case.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
