    "created_at": "2023-09-08T13:39:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-09-08T13:39:16Z",
    "body": "if the crux of this issue is \"can we just allow divs inside buttons\", then sure, go nuts.  people do it all the time and while it used to cause some issues for some screen readers, it mostly seems ok these days.  But also, as Domenic mentioned, span is a thing too, so, maybe that's fine enough to expect developers to know when to use a div vs span?  \r\n\r\nBut, if the goal is to expand beyond that - allowing more content types as descendants of buttons, then we venture into the current problematic territories of `summary` (i won't retread that conversation here) but also `a`.  Hyperlinks being allowed to contain more than phrasing content has it's own problems - [here's just one blog post that touches on why](https://adrianroselli.com/2020/02/block-links-cards-clickable-regions-etc.html#Links).  It can be useful in some situations, and it can be absolutely bonkers broken UX in others... for instance if one were to wrap an entire table in a hyperlink... that's gross - having the accessible name of that link be a flattened text string of the entirety of all the tabular data.  And if you think \"oh well, put an aria-label on that link\" then cool, now the browser has sent a signal to the AT of \"ignore everything in this hyperlink - someone gave it an aria-label which means the content isn't important\".   So, horrible UX for AT, but valid HTML.  Let's make sure to not let buttons do that too, please.\r\n\r\n\r\n",
