    "created_at": "2023-09-29T21:44:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-09-29T21:44:29Z",
    "body": "> Can you ask this person why a span does not work for them? For better or for worse, we have both, and have decided they have different use cases. If we want to try to erase the distinction between them and all div wherever span is allowed, we could consider doing so, but I don't think that we should do it as a one-off for button.\r\n\r\nClearly a `<span>` can be made to work in any case that a `<div>` is used here. It's just that developers know to reach for `<div>` when they want a generic block-level element, **except when inside a button**. That's just another exception to the rule which is confusing and takes mental bandwidth. It's like English's \"i before e\" rule - there are so many exceptions that you can't use the rule. Since it seems like there's no actual reason for the `<div>` limitation, other than history, perhaps we can relax it?\r\n\r\nI'd argue the same for other elements that restrict to phrasing content for no \"good\" reason.",
